<BaseLayout
  title="Research | Evan Hobson"
  description="Cattle and cheatgrass, feral horses, and a notebook of unfinished movement and fire work."
>
  <section class="page">
    <h1>Research</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      I work in April Hulet's Rangeland Ecology Lab at BYU. Most of what I do starts with GPS
      collar data and a question about where animals go.
    </p>

    <h2>Current</h2>
    <div class="list">
      <article class="item" id="cattle-rsf">
        <h3>Cattle and cheatgrass</h3>
        <p class="meta">Lead author · manuscript with coauthors</p>
        <p>
          One way to fight cheatgrass is to turn cattle out in the fall to graze it down. That only
          works if the cows actually go to the cheatgrass. Cows on the Spring Hills allotment near
          Malad, Idaho, wore GPS collars during fall grazing in 2021 and 2022 as part of a
          targeted-grazing study.
        </p>
        <p>
          There are several satellite-based maps of cheatgrass cover, and they don't agree. I fit
          the same habitat-selection model three times, changing only which cheatgrass map goes
          in, to see how much the answer depends on that choice.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2>On hold</h2>
    <div class="list">
      <article class="item" id="states">
        <h3>What the cows were doing</h3>
        <p>
          The collars also had accelerometers, so each location comes with a guess at behavior:
          grazing, resting or walking. I want to know whether cows pick places differently
          depending on what they're doing there. This is waiting on the original behavior labels
          from the study that collected the data.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item" id="horses">
        <h3>Feral horses in northwestern Nevada</h3>
        <p class="meta">Lead author</p>
        <p>
          Older GPS data from 11 horses, with fixes every 30 minutes. I measured how far they moved
          each day and how much ground they covered, and how that changed with season and water.
          Paused until there's a clearer plan for the paper.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2 id="notebook">Notebook</h2>
    <p>
      Projects I took partway and stopped. Most stopped because I found someone had already done
      the important part.
    </p>
    <div class="list">
      <article class="item">
        <h3>From individual step choices to population maps</h3>
        <p>
          Step-selection models are fit to individual animals, but managers want a map for the
          population. I worked out how much is lost by averaging individuals, and tested it on Ya
          Ha Tinda elk data. Fieberg and others (2009) had already covered the core idea. One puzzle
          is left: the plain average-animal map predicted held-out elk better than the corrected
          ones.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item">
        <h3>How often should a collar take a fix?</h3>
        <p>
          I tried to build a calculator for choosing a GPS fix interval for step-selection studies.
          The cattle data suggested a best interval. It didn't hold up on fisher or zebra data, so I
          stopped. Along the way I found two bugs in the <code>amt</code> R package.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item">
        <h3>How many behavior states?</h3>
        <p>
          Hidden Markov models of movement let you choose the number of behavior states, and the
          usual criteria pick too many. With the cattle accelerometer labels I could check the
          extra states against what the cows were doing. Pohle and others (2017) had already made
          the main point, so this became a paragraph in the behavior paper instead of its own
          project.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item">
        <h3>Rebuilding gridMET's fire danger index</h3>
        <p>
          gridMET publishes a daily energy release component (ERC), a standard fire-danger number,
          but doesn't say exactly how it's computed. I rewrote it from the 1978 National Fire
          Danger Rating System equations. It matches on dry days. To match on wet days I had to set
          fine-fuel moisture to 16%, which isn't documented anywhere I could find.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="item">
        <h3>A fuel layer for Great Basin annual grass</h3>
        <p>
          The plan was a map of annual-grass fuel condition that carries over litter from earlier
          years. BLM already makes something close to it (Smith 2022), so I stopped there.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <h2 id="field">Fieldwork</h2>
    <div class="list">
      <article class="item">
        <h3>Summer 2026, Rangeland Ecology Lab</h3>
        <p>
          Vegetation monitoring for SageSTEP and a targeted sheep-grazing study in Idaho:
          line-point intercept, gap intercept, plant density, biomass, shrub volume and soil
          stability.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="item">
        <h3>2024 to 2025, Wildlife Ecology Lab</h3>
        <p>
          Pygmy rabbit camera and vegetation surveys, mule deer collar retrievals and mortality
          investigations, and aging deer from tooth cementum.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
